WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.708
Triage turns scanner output into engineering decisions.

2
00:00:03.708 --> 00:00:17.101
A reviewer confirms whether a finding is relevant, whether the affected code is reachable, whether the source is realistic, whether the sink has security impact, and whether existing controls change the risk.

3
00:00:17.101 --> 00:00:26.938
This step matters because raw tool severity may not reflect the application’s architecture, deployment model, data sensitivity, or business exposure.

4
00:00:26.938 --> 00:00:29.056
Context drives prioritization.

5
00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:35.110
A risky pattern in an internet-facing administrative workflow may deserve urgent attention.

6
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:40.331
The same pattern in unreachable test-only code may have a different disposition.

7
00:00:40.331 --> 00:00:50.092
Data sensitivity, user roles, tenant boundaries, exploitability at a high level, compensating controls, and business impact all influence severity.

8
00:00:50.092 --> 00:00:55.162
Prioritization should combine tool output with engineering and risk context.

9
00:00:55.162 --> 00:00:58.037
Grouping related findings can reduce noise.

10
00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:07.874
Many reports may share one root cause, such as a missing validation helper, an unsafe wrapper, a legacy query pattern, or a framework configuration issue.

11
00:01:07.874 --> 00:01:13.927
Fixing the root cause may be more effective than treating every instance as a separate problem.

12
00:01:13.927 --> 00:01:21.267
Good triage also assigns owners and records why decisions were made, especially when risk is accepted or deferred.

13
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:23.688
Documentation is part of the control.

14
00:01:23.688 --> 00:01:29.363
A decision to fix, suppress, accept, defer, or reclassify a finding should be traceable.

15
00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:37.914
The record does not need to be burdensome, but it should explain the evidence, owner, severity, expected action, and validation plan.

16
00:01:37.914 --> 00:01:42.302
Prioritization is not about ignoring low-priority issues forever.

17
00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:47.750
It is about sequencing work so the highest-risk problems are addressed with clarity.
